<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have significantly advanced multimodal understanding but still struggle with efficiently processing high-resolution images. Recent approaches partition high-resolution images into multiple sub-images, dramatically increasing the number of visual tokens and causing exponential computational overhead during inference. To address these limitations, we propose a training-free token pruning strategy, Pyramid Token Pruning (PTP), that integrates bottom-up visual saliency at both region and token levels with top-down instruction-guided importance. Inspired by human visual attention mechanisms, PTP selectively retains more tokens from visually salient regions and further leverages textual instructions to pinpoint tokens most relevant to specific multimodal tasks. Extensive experiments across 13 diverse benchmarks demonstrate that our method substantially reduces computational overhead and inference latency with minimal performance loss.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Instruction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have advanced multimodal understanding by combining large language models with visual encoders. While early LVLMs like LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and MiniGPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> demonstrated impressive image understanding capabilities, they process images at low resolutions and struggle to capture fine-grained visual details <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. To address this limitation, recent LVLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> have introduced dynamic image partitioning, where a high-resolution image is divided into multiple sub-images accompanied by a downsampled global image. While this strategy preserves significantly more visual detail (e.g. yielding up to a 15<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy gain when increasing input resolution from 336 to 1344 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>), they significantly increases the number of visual tokens. The excessive tokens drastically increase inference cost – slowing throughput, raising latency, and straining GPU memory. Yet, paradoxically, only a small fraction of the visual tokens truly influence the LVLM’s output. For instance, in LLaVA-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, the average attention to image tokens after a few layers is merely  0.2<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> of that on text tokens. This suggests tremendous redundancy: a high-resolution LVLM spends the bulk of its computation on visual tokens that the LLM largely ignores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate this inefficiency, researchers have explored a variety of visual token compression strategies within LVLMs. FastV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> prunes “unimportant” image tokens dynamically based on their attention scores, effectively removing low-attended tokens after a certain transformer layer. LLaVA-PruMerge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> retains the most critical tokens and merges the remaining ones into those, rather than discarding them outright. VTW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> simplifies the extreme case by dropping all visual tokens beyond a chosen layer of the LLM, under the premise that vision features become less needed in deep layers. Token Packer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> constructs point-to-region correspondences to facilitate token compression. There are also works that attempted to compress visual inputs via learned modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> or using bayesian optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. While these approaches can reduce token counts, they suffer notable shortcomings. First, their methods are usually heuristic (e.g. based on attention magnitude or similarity computation), which inadvertently discard useful visual cues. For example, aggressive schemes like VTW may remove tokens that are actually needed for generating accurate responses. Second, most of these methods usually ignore the task instruction when pruning visual tokens, so they cannot distinguish tokens that are unimportant in general from tokens that are closely related to the textual question. Third, some of these methods tend to rely on additional training or fine-tuning of the LVLM which could limit their applicability across different models. These shortcomings may explain why the current pruning methods often perform no better (and sometimes worse) than naive random dropping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. It suggests that current criteria for "important" tokens are misguided, often preserving the wrong tokens while removing the right ones. These observations highlight the need for a smarter pruning approach that truly prioritizes the tokens carrying essential information for the task at hand.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose a training-free Pyramid Token Pruning (PTP) framework that addresses the above challenges by integrating bottom-up visual saliency at multiple scales with top-down instruction-driven importance signals. Our method is training-free, meaning it operates as a drop-in inference mechanism without requiring any additional model training or fine-tuning. The core idea is a pyramid of pruning stages that reflect how humans process visual information: from coarse regions to fine details, guided by both the image’s salient content and the viewer’s task. We have two primary goals: (1) Significantly reduce computational overhead by pruning the vast number of redundant visual tokens before they tax the LLM, and (2) Precisely preserve informative tokens so that the model’s performance remains virtually intact.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15704v1/figure1-intro-v3.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:305/148;" width="305" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">PTP Pipeline</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15704v1/radar-final.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:157/157;" width="157" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Performence Radar Chart</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) Pipeline of the proposed Pyramid Token Pruning (PTP) method. (b) Performance comparison with the baseline model and other token pruning methods across 13 LVLM benchmarks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Instruction ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a), our method first draws inspiration from visual cognition, where human attention first locks onto salient regions of a scene and then hones in on salient details <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In the context of high-resolution LVLMs, a single salient object may be split into multiple sub-images. To mimic human-like focus, PTP performs region-level pruning as a first stage. We compute a saliency score for each sub-image to estimate how “visually interesting” that region is in general. PTP then allocates token budget across the sub-images based on the saliency score. This bottom-up regional focus aligns with how human would allocate more attention (like "tokens") to visually striking parts of an image. Next, at a finer granularity, we perform token-level pruning within each tile. Even inside a salient image region, not every token is equally informative. For each sub-image, PTP measures the saliency of the visual tokens in that tile. This two-level pyramid (region-first, then token) ensures a balanced, coarse-to-fine pruning: we first apply a higher token pruning ratio to uninteresting regions, and then mark less useful tokens from the interesting regions, all without losing the critical content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While bottom-up saliency is crucial, relying on it alone is risky because the model’s goal is to response a specific instruction which may require information that isn’t salient in a purely visual sense. Therefore, PTP augments the saliency-based pruning with an instruction-guided filtering stage. We incorporate the textual query as a context to inform which visual tokens are likely to be relevant to the current task. Therefore, after the initial saliency-based pruning, we perform a lightweight instruction relevance check: any token that is strongly related to the query is marked as important, which ensures that no instruction-critical token is inadvertently dropped. The top-down guidance complements the bottom-up filter, leading to a more task-aware pruning process. Notably, all of this is achieved without any retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the effectiveness of the proposed PTP framework through extensive experiments on 13 diverse benchmarks, as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Instruction ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b), using the InternVL2-2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> as a baseline. Results show that PTP can accelerate inference dramatically with negligible performance loss: we manage to prune a large fraction of visual tokens (e.g. reducing token count by 50<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>) while keeping accuracy within a few points of the unpruned model on all tasks. In many cases, the performance drop is under 0.5<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that PTP preserves the informative visual contents needed by the LVLM. These efficiency gains translate to significantly lower latency and memory usage in practice, addressing the key bottlenecks of high-resolution LVLM deployment. We also conduct detailed ablation studies to understand the contribution of each component. Interestingly, we find that the importance of saliency-based vs. instruction-based pruning is task-dependent. For OCR-related tasks, the bottom-up saliency alone suffices to identify redundant tokens. However, for scene understanding tasks like RealWorldQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the contribution of instruction-based pruning becomes more significant. We believe these findings not only demonstrate PTP’s effectiveness, but also shed light on the broader question of what visual tokens an LVLM truly needs for different problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our work makes the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom-up Token Pruning:</span> We propose a novel visual token pruning mechanism that operates in a multi-level “pyramid” fashion. By leveraging bottom-up visual saliency at both region and token levels, our method effectively identifies and removes redundant visual tokens, significantly improving high-resolution LVLM inference efficiency without any model modifications or re-training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-down Token Pruning:</span> We introduce a top-down, instruction-aware pruning mechanism that complements visual saliency with textual context. By incorporating the user’s query or task instructions into the pruning criteria, our method strikes a superior balance between efficiency and performance, carefully preserving tokens that are relevant to the specific task (which purely bottom-up methods would overlook).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comprehensive evaluation and insights:</span> We effectively integrate the bottom-up and top-down mechanisms, forming the PTP token pruning framework. When applying to InternVL2-2B, it achieves substantial speed-ups with minimal performance degradation across 13 diverse LVLM benchmarks without any model training. Furthermore, we validate the biases in the dependency of different task types on visual saliency and instruction-guided cues.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Development of Large Vision-Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large vision-language models (LVLMs) integrate vision encoders with large language models to enable multimodal understanding. Early models (LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, ShareGPT4V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, instructblip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>) demonstrated this paradigm by connecting a pre-trained visual encoder (e.g. CLIP-based ViTs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> ) to an LLM, allowing open-ended visual question answering and dialogue. Subsequent LVLMs further improve performance baselines by scaling up model and data size. For instance, InternVL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> series attempt to align a 6B-parameter vision backbone with large language models, closing the gap toward proprietary multimodal systems such as GPT4-V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. More recent variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> push toward ultra-high-resolution inputs to capture fine-grained details. These methods employ sub-image partitioning algorithms to divide the original image into smaller segments that can be processed by the visual encoder, offering impressive fine-grained visual perception. However, encoding a high-resolution image as thousands of tokens poses significant efficiency challenges for inference, motivating strategies to reduce the visual token count without significantly sacrificing performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Token Purning in Large Vision-Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A range of visual token compression techniques have been explored to tackle the computational burden of lengthy image token sequences. Early approaches treat token reduction as a feature merging problem. For example, LLaVA-PruMerge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and TokenPacker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> combine neighboring patch tokens with high similarities. However, these methods need to perform further model training and their merging module fail to consider instruction guidance. As a result, they cannot be directly applied across different LVLM architectures and may weaken the instruction-related visual information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, more adaptive token pruning methods have been proposed. FastV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> prunes visual tokens by learning adaptive early-layer attention patterns in the LLM (achieving sizable FLOPs reductions with minimal loss), while VTW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> withdraws all image tokens after a certain transformer layer once their information has been absorbed into textual representations. Other methods employ multi-stage or search-based compression schedules: PDrop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> progressively drops a fraction of tokens at successive layers, balancing efficiency and accuracy. G-Search and P-Sigmoid form a dual-strategy method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, where G-Search greedily identifies the minimal token set per layer, and P-Sigmoid refines this with a sigmoid-based bayesian optimization to approximate optimal layerwise retention ratios. In parallel, instruction-aware techniques explicitly aim to keep tokens relevant to the input query: for example, LVPruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> prunes away visual tokens deemed irrelevant to the question by aligning token importance with the textual instruction or dialogue context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While these methods have shown effectiveness, they have certain limitations. Many require additional training, fine-tuning or carefully tuned hyperparameters to identify important tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and they often rely on a single indicator of token importance. This can lead to overfitting to particular tokens that correlate with the instruction even if those tokens do not ultimately impact the answer. Our method addresses these issues by fusing bottom-up visual importance cues with top-down instruction signals in a training-free pruning framework, achieving efficient token reduction without retraining the LVLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ InternVL2-2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> as the implementation framework. It adopts a standard high-resolution image processing pipeline. A high-resolution image is first divided by a dynamic cropping module into several sub-images along with a thumbnail of the entire image, all of which are resized to a target resolution of 448. Then the vision encoder encodes each image into a set of patch embeddings and a global CLS embedding. The patch embeddings are then mapped through a vision-language projector into the LLM’s embedding space. The LLM processes these visual tokens alongside text tokens to produce responses. The proposed Pyramid Token Pruning (PTP) method operates as a plug-and-play module between the visual encoder and the LLM, filtering out the uninformative visual tokens without any training. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, PTP leverages a three-tier importance estimation to identify the most relevent visual tokens. By pruning tokens based on these important scores, PTP can significantly speed up multimodal inference while maintaining model fidelity. Importantly, PTP’s design is model-agnostic and does not require additional training or finetuning, making it easy to integrate into existing LVLM pipelines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15704v1/Figure-method-V2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/351;" width="476" height="351" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>(a) Overall workflow of PTP. (b) Top-down instruction-guided importance computation. (c) Bottom-up token-level importance computation. (d) Bottom-up region-level importance computation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Region-Level Importance Scoring</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first partition the input image into an <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> grid of sub-images (local regions), and also generate a global thumbnail by downsampling the entire image. This yields <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\cdot n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\cdot n</annotation></semantics></math> local regions plus 1 global image, for a total of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\cdot n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\cdot n+1</annotation></semantics></math> image tiles. Each region (sub-image) is independently fed through the visual encoder, producing a sequence of patch tokens and a [CLS] token for that region. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{cls}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐜𝐥𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{cls}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the CLS embedding of the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th region, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{cls}^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐜𝐥𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{cls}^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the CLS embedding of the global image. We quantify the region-level importance of each local region <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by measuring its semantic alignment with the whole image. Specifically, we compute the cosine similarity between the region’s CLS embedding and the global CLS embedding:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\cos\left(\mathbf{cls}^{(i)},\ \mathbf{cls}^{(g)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐜𝐥𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐜𝐥𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\cos\left(\mathbf{cls}^{(i)},\ \mathbf{cls}^{(g)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, a sub-region whose representation is very similar to the global image representation (high <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) likely contains content central to the image’s overall context. We then apply a softmax normalization across all sub-region scores <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a distribution of region importance. Given a desired overall purning ratio <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we allocate each sub-image a token budget of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=(1-r)\cdot T\cdot\mathrm{softmax}(a_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=(1-r)\cdot T\cdot\mathrm{softmax}(a_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of visual tokens. In this case, regions with higher saliency can retain more tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Token-Level Bottom-Up Scoring</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, within each region, PTP assesses the importance of individual patch tokens using a bottom-up attention-based score. The idea is to identify which patches most strongly influence the region’s overall representation. We leverage the self-attention mechanism of the ViT for this purpose. From the transformer layer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of the visual encoder, we extract the attention weights from the region’s CLS token to every patch token in that region. Formally, for region <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the bottom-up score of its <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th patch token is defined as the CLS-to-patch attention weight at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(i)}=\mathrm{Attn}_{\mathrm{cls}\rightarrow\mathrm{patch}}^{(L)}(j)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Attn</mi><mrow><mi>cls</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>patch</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(i)}=\mathrm{Attn}_{\mathrm{cls}\rightarrow\mathrm{patch}}^{(L)}(j).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Attn}^{(L)}_{\text{cls}\rightarrow\text{patch}}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mtext>cls</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>patch</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Attn}^{(L)}_{\text{cls}\rightarrow\text{patch}}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the attention coefficient that the CLS token assigns to patch <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer. A high score <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents that the patch <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> contributed strongly to the regional representation, indicating that patch carries important visual information for that region. By computing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for all tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in region <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a ranking of local patches by their intrinsic visual significance in that region.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Instruction-Guided Top-Down Scoring</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the above scores capture image-driven saliency, our method also incorporates top-down guidance from the text instruction or query. After the visual tokens are aligned with the LLM’s embedding space, they are concatenated with the textual instruction tokens and fed together into the LLM. We check the attention scores allocated by the textual tokens to the visual tokens at the LLM’s early layers to determine which visual tokens are most relevant to the text. In particular, we inspect the attention weights from each instruction token to each visual token at the second transformer block of the LLM. For each visual token <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we define its instruction-guided importance <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum attention it receives from any of the query’s tokens:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}=\max_{q\in Q}\mathrm{Attn}_{q\rightarrow j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Attn</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}=\max_{q\in Q}\mathrm{Attn}_{q\rightarrow j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all instruction token indices. In other words, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> measures how strongly at least one word in the instruction attends to visual token <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. If the user’s prompt focuses on a particular object or region, the corresponding visual tokens will achieve high <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> scores. By obtaining <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all candidate tokens, PTP captures text-conditioned saliency, ensuring that the visual tokens crucial for answering the query are prioritized.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Pyramid Token Pruning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we combine the above signals into a unified importance measure to perform the actual token selection. For token j in sub-image i, the set of scores (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{b_{j}^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{b_{j}^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{c_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{c_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is respectively normalized to the [0,1] range to make their magnitudes comparable. The region-level score <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to determine the regional token budget <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The scores <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{b_{j}^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{b_{j}^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{c_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{c_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> are used to compute token-level importance:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}=\alpha c_{j}+(1-\alpha)b_{j}^{(i)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}=\alpha c_{j}+(1-\alpha)b_{j}^{(i)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that trades off instruction guidance versus visual content in the token ranking. Setting <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to purely image-driven pruning (using <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>), while <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that only the instruction cue is used. In practice, we empirically choose an <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> value to balance both criteria. Given region <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s budget <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we select the top-<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in that region ranked by <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (tokens with largest <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are retained). For the global thumbnail image: its tokens are ranked based on scores <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the top-<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens are retained, where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the global region’s token budget equals to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\cdot T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\cdot T</annotation></semantics></math>). By pruning away all the unselected patch tokens , only the most important visual tokens from each region and the global image are retained. These retained visual tokens are then passed through the vision-language projector and fed into the LLM along with the textual embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In effect, PTP produces a shorter visual token sequence that still preserves the critical information needed for the current task. Our pyramidal pruning framework, which operates first at the region level, then at the token level with both bottom-up and top-down signals, efficiently filtrate the most relevant visual content. This leads to much more efficient inference with minimal impact on model’s performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Architecture.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ InternVL2-2B as the baseline LVLM to demonstrate the effectiveness of our method. The final model includes an InternLM2-1.8B as the LLM backbone, a two-layer MLP as the vision-language projector, and an InternViT-300M as the vision encoder.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the effectiveness and efficiency of our proposed token pruning method, we adopt 13 widely used benchmarks for LVLMs, which cover a diverse range of task types and are grouped into four categories. The General VQA category includes AI2D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, SQA-I <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, VizWiz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and GQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. The OCR category consists of Text-VQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and Chart-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. The Comprehensive category includes MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, MMB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, MM-Vet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, SEED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and MM-Star <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, which are designed to test general multimodal understanding and reasoning abilities. The Others category covers POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and RealWorldQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, where POPE tests object existence hallucinations and RealWorldQA tests real-world scene understanding. Our experiments were conducted using a single NVIDIA L20 GPU with 48GB of VRAM.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results and Analyses</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Main Result</h4>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of different token pruning methods based on InternVL2-2B across 13 LVLM benchmarks.</span> "Avg. Acc." denotes the average performance reservation relative to the baseline model. The top-2 best scores are highlighted in bold and underline respectively.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
