</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2.1 Main Result ‣ 4.2 Results and Analyses ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the performance of various token pruning methods built upon the InternVL2-2B across 13 LVLM benchmarks spanning four task categories. The proposed PTP achieves the best overall trade-off between performance and efficiency among all evaluated methods. It retains 99.6% of the original InternVL2-2B model’s accuracy while reducing computational cost from 8.42 to 5.71 TFLOPs, representing a 32.2% reduction in FLOPs with only a 0.4% accuracy loss. Compared to other pruning strategies, PTP consistently delivers superior or competitive accuracy across diverse tasks. For instance, on Chart-QA and POPE, PTP achieves 69.8 and 86.1 respectively, outperforming all other pruned methods and nearly matching the baseline. On MMStar, a challenging benchmark for multimodal reasoning, PTP reaches 49.6, which is the highest among all competitors and only 0.4% below the baseline. Moreover, on comprehensive benchmarks such as MME, PTP even exceeds the baseline, suggesting that pruning noisy or redundant tokens can enhance signal fidelity in some cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to prior methods like FastV (95.8%, 6.03 TFlops) and GSearch (98.1%, 5.89 TFlops), PTP achieves both higher accuracy (99.6%) and lower inference cost (5.71 TFlops) at the same retention ratio, demonstrating significant improvements on multiple datasets. These results confirm PTP as a robust and generalizable pruning mechanism that maintains task performance while significantly reducing computational overhead. It is noteworthy that Random pruning and Spatially-R (spatially uniform random pruning) even outperform carefully designed methods such as FastV on tasks like SEED and GQA. This highlights the current challenge in token pruning: designing a method that is both generalizable and effective across diverse tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
