</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.2 Vision Encoder Layer Selection ‣ 4.2 Results and Analyses ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the results of the PTP method when using different ViT layers to compute the bottom-up token-level importance scores. It shows that an intermediate layer offers the best overall performance: using features from layer 11 yields the highest aggregate accuracy across most benchmarks. Specifically, layer 11 achieves 93.2 in SQA-I, 29.1 in VizWiz, and 56.8 in GQA - each of the best results in its respective data set. And it ranks within the top-2 in MME (1880.1), MMStar (49.6) and POPE (86.1). These results suggest that layer 11 is a reliable and broadly effective default configuration for PTP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we observe task-dependent preferences across layers: OCR-related tasks such as Text-VQA and Chart-QA favor earlier layers (0–3), likely due to their emphasis on local spatial detail, while tasks involving scene-level reasoning such as RealWorldQA show improved performance when features are extracted from deeper layers. These trends are consistent with prior findings in earlier work, which demonstrated that different visual layers contribute differently to various multimodal task types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Additional analysis is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Analysis of Layer-wise Preferences in the Vision Encoder ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Visualization of Attention Head Selection Strategy ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

