<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2.3 Hyperparameter 𝛼 ‣ 4.2 Results and Analyses ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we investigate the effect of the hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which controls the trade-off between bottom-up token importance <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and top-down instruction-related importance in the PTP framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results show that setting <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> yields the best or second-best performance on most benchmarks in the General VQA and Comprehensive categories. This suggests that the tasks which require understanding a broad range of visual scenes and following varied instructions can benefit from a balanced integration of visual saliency and instruction guidance. In other words, a balanced weighting between bottom-up visual features and top-down question cues appears to be effective for general-purpose VQA and comprehensive reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, for OCR-focused tasks such as Text-VQA and Chart-QA, we observe that smaller values of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 0.2) lead to better performance. These tasks involve recognizing and reasoning about text or numbers in images, where the crucial information tends to be highly salient in the visual domain (for example, printed labels or chart annotations). Under these conditions, emphasizing the bottom-up importance score (by using a lower <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) can better preserve the image tokens relevant to the text regions, whereas the language model’s text-based attention may not able to highlight. A similar pattern is seen on the POPE benchmark. It is likely due to that the misleading prompt tokens in POPE can corrupt the top-down attention, making the model rely more on visual cues to locate relevant information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the RealWorldQA task shows improved performance at higher <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values (0.8). RealWorldQA often involves complex, open-ended queries that are semantically related to the visual elements. In this scenario, the textual instruction could provide more useful guidance than the bottom-up visual saliency. Therefore, emphasizing the top-down signal (high <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) enables the model to preserve the most important visual tokens, which further explain the superior performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these findings imply that different types of VQA tasks benefit from different balances of bottom-up and top-down influence. Tasks with substantial textual or structured visual content prefer lower <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values (relying more on visual saliency), whereas more open-domain or instruction-driven tasks prefer higher <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values. This observation suggests that a dynamic fusion strategy, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is adapted based on task characteristics or input signals, could be a promising direction for future work. Nonetheless, given its consistently strong performance across diverse benchmarks, we recommend <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> as a default setting for general-purpose use of the PTP framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4 </span>Ablation Study</h4>

<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
