</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4.2 Results and Analyses ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the results of the ablation study which isolate the contribution of each component. The complete PTP method serves as a performance baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Removing the region-based token allocation causes a 1.65% average performance drop. This indicates that considering the importance difference among local regions is critical. Further omitting the token-level bottom-up score <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> raises the drop to 2.33%. This confirms that bottom-up saliency cues are valuable for selecting informative tokens. Removing the instruction-guided score c (while also omitting region allocation) yields a much larger drop. This highlights the importance of top-down, instruction-driven pruning: using textual instruction to filter visual tokens greatly improves the task-relevance of the preserved visual information. As a control, uniformly removing tokens at random produces a 7.27% drop, far worse than any other setting. These ablation results demonstrate that omitting any component noticeably degrades performance, confirming that the combined PTP design is efficient and effective in retaining critical visual information. Supplementary ablation study is presented in the Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Supplementary Ablation Study ‣ Training-Free Pyramid Token Pruning for Efficient Large Vision-Language Models via Region, Token, and Instruction-Guided Importance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, we have introduced PTP, a novel training-free pyramid token pruning framework for high-resolution LVLMs. It hierarchically integrates region-level saliency scoring with bottom-up token score and top-down instruction-guided importance to filtrate important visual tokens. This simple yet effective design yields substantial efficiency gains: on InternVL2-2B, PTP preserves 99.6% of the original accuracy while reducing the model’s FLOPs by 32.2%, and it consistently outperforms or matches existing token-pruning methods across 13 diverse benchmarks. Extensive ablations verify the robustness of our approach: all three PTP components are indispensable for maintaining model performance. In future work, we plan to further refine this framework by developing fully training-free, adaptive <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-fusion strategies that can automatically balance these importance cues for even greater efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
