<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results indicate that the "max" operation is more effective in focusing on instruction-relevant object information, whereas the “mean” operation is more likely to be diluted by irrelevant instruction tokens, leading to suboptimal attention allocation. Therefore, adopting the "max" strategy for calculating instruction-guided importance enhances the model’s ability to attend to the most critical visual features specified by the instruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Limitation</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a training-free method, Pyramid Token Pruning (PTP), to efficiently identify key tokens in high-resolution LVLMs. One limitation is that different task types require different balances between bottom-up visual saliency scores and top-down instruction-guided importance scores, typically controlled by a fixed interpolation factor <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. While PTP adopts a universal fixed <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, future work may explore integrating an adaptive <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> mechanism into the PTP framework to achieve better performance. In addition, our current evaluation lacks experiments on a broader range of LVLM architectures, which would help further validate the generality and effectiveness of our approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Broader impacts and safety issues</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work aims to improve the computational efficiency of high-resolution LVLMs through a training-free token pruning framework, which can significantly reduce inference cost without sacrificing accuracy. This advancement has the potential to enable broader deployment of multimodal AI assistants in resource-constrained environments, such as mobile devices, real-time applications, and educational tools. However, as with all LVLM-based systems, our method inherits the risks associated with the underlying language model, including hallucinated or misleading outputs. These issues may lead to incorrect or potentially harmful responses, particularly when models are used in sensitive contexts. Future work should consider coupling efficiency enhancements with robust safety monitoring and hallucination mitigation mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
